ANGLE: GL_QCOM_motion_estimation as a compute shader

Games use Qualcomm's frame generation library, which needs this extension
to find how each block of the picture moved between two frames. Qualcomm
does it in fixed-function hardware; here a compute shader in UtilsVk
searches for each 16x16 block of the reference image in the target image:
every other offset and pixel over +-16 pixels, then every pixel around the
best match, then a parabola fit for the sub-pixel part. A motion that
barely beats no motion counts as none. The vectors go through a buffer into
the RGBA16F output, so the output texture needs no storage usage.

The entry points, enum, proc table, context and validation declarations
and the extension flag are written by hand in the generated files:
Android's ANGLE source has no Khronos registry to run the generators on.

